---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

export const frontmatter = {
  title: 'BatchNorm vs No-BatchNorm: Why It Works',
  pubDate: '2024-08-26',
};
---

<BlogPostLayout frontmatter={frontmatter}>
  <p class="mb-6 text-gray-700">Batch Normalization (BN) shows up in almost every modern neural network, usually with little explanation of <i>why</i> it helps. This is a visual, beginner-friendly walkthrough. We train the same small image classifier twice—once with BN, once without—and watch what happens to the numbers flowing through the network. If you've seen a basic neural network and gradient descent, you have enough background to follow along.</p>

  <div class="mb-6 p-4 rounded border bg-blue-50 text-blue-900">
    <b>TL;DR.</b> BN takes the numbers inside each layer and, for every mini-batch, rescales them to a tidy range (roughly mean 0, spread 1), then lets the network learn two small parameters (<i>γ</i>, <i>β</i>) to readjust if needed. In our experiment this made training much more stable and accurate—most dramatically at a high learning rate (lr = 0.2), where the BN model reached ≈ 89% accuracy versus ≈ 75% without it.
  </div>

  <section class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">What BatchNorm actually does</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">A few terms first. When you train a network you don't feed it one example at a time but a small group at once—a <b>mini-batch</b> (128 images here). As data passes through a layer it produces <b>activations</b> (the layer's output numbers), and in a convolutional layer these are organized into <b>channels</b> (feature maps). For each channel, BN looks at all the activations in the current mini-batch, computes their mean and variance, and standardizes them to mean 0 / variance 1. It then applies a learnable <b>scale</b> (<i>γ</i>) and <b>shift</b> (<i>β</i>), so the network can adjust or even undo the normalization if that turns out to help. During training it uses each batch's own statistics; at test time it switches to a running average collected during training, so a prediction doesn't depend on which other images happen to share its batch.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">In symbols, for one channel <code>c</code> with <code>m</code> examples in the batch:</p>
    <div class="bg-white rounded border p-4 mb-4 text-base md:text-lg leading-relaxed">
      <b>Per channel c:</b><br/>
      <code>μ<sub>c</sub> = (1/m) Σ<sub>i=1..m</sub> x<sub>i,c</sub></code><br/>
      <code>σ<sub>c</sub><sup>2</sup> = (1/m) Σ<sub>i=1..m</sub> (x<sub>i,c</sub> − μ<sub>c</sub>)²</code><br/>
      <code>x̂<sub>i,c</sub> = (x<sub>i,c</sub> − μ<sub>c</sub>) / √(σ<sub>c</sub><sup>2</sup> + ε)</code><br/>
      <code>y<sub>i,c</sub> = γ<sub>c</sub> · x̂<sub>i,c</sub> + β<sub>c</sub></code>
      <p class="mt-2 text-gray-600 text-sm md:text-base">Here <code>ε</code> is a small constant for numerical stability; <code>γ</code> (scale) and <code>β</code> (shift) are learned like ordinary parameters.</p>
    </div>
    <details class="mb-4 group">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer select-none font-medium text-gray-800 group-open:underline">Tiny numeric example (one channel)</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-gray-700 text-sm md:text-base space-y-2">
        <p>Suppose a batch (m = 4) gives channel values: <code>[2, 4, 6, 8]</code>.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside">
          <li>Mean <code>μ = (2+4+6+8)/4 = 5</code></li>
          <li>Variance <code>σ^2 = ((2-5)^2 + (4-5)^2 + (6-5)^2 + (8-5)^2)/4 = (9+1+1+9)/4 = 5</code></li>
          <li>Std <code>σ = √5 ≈ 2.236</code></li>
          <li>Normalized <code>x̂ ≈ [(2−5)/2.236, (4−5)/2.236, (6−5)/2.236, (8−5)/2.236] ≈ [−1.34, −0.45, 0.45, 1.34]</code></li>
          <li>If <code>γ = 1.1</code>, <code>β = -0.2</code> then outputs <code>y ≈ [-1.67, -0.69, 0.30, 1.27]</code></li>
        </ul>
        <p>The raw spread <code>[2..8]</code> has been standardized, then gently re‑scaled and shifted; subsequent layers now see a stable distribution across steps.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
    <div class="p-4 rounded border bg-emerald-50 text-emerald-900 text-sm md:text-base">
      <b>Why it helps (the intuition):</b> (1) it stops activations from slowly growing or shrinking out of control as they pass through many layers; (2) it makes the optimization surface smoother, so each gradient step is more predictable; (3) because of that, you can safely use a larger learning rate and train faster; (4) each batch's statistics are slightly random, which adds a little noise that acts like mild regularization (similar in spirit to dropout).
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm md:text-base text-gray-600"><b>A note on the "why".</b> The original BN paper explained the benefit as reducing <i>internal covariate shift</i> (ICS)—the idea that, as earlier layers keep updating, the distribution of inputs to later layers keeps shifting, forcing them to constantly readapt. That story is intuitive and still widely repeated, but later work (Santurkar et al., 2018, <i>How Does Batch Normalization Help Optimization?</i>) showed it is largely wrong: BN doesn't really reduce this shift, and its main effect is making the loss surface smoother and easier to optimize. So read the plots below as showing <i>what</i> BN changes in the signals—not as proof of the textbook "covariate shift" explanation.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-2">The cast and the stage</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-700 space-y-1">
      <li><b>Data:</b> CIFAR‑10 (32×32), light aug (crop/flip).</li>
      <li><b>Model:</b> a small 5-block convolutional network. Each block is convolution → ReLU; the BN version adds a BN layer right before each ReLU. A small linear classifier (the "head") sits on top.</li>
      <li><b>Training:</b> everything is identical between the two models—same starting weights, same optimizer, same schedule—except whether BN is on. We try two learning rates: a gentle one (lr = 0.02) and an aggressive one (lr = 0.2). The <b>learning rate</b> is how big a step the optimizer takes per update; BN's advantage is easiest to see when we push it hard.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 text-base md:text-lg">The goal isn't a record-breaking accuracy—it's a clean look at how BN changes the signals inside the network.</p>
    <div class="mt-4 p-3 rounded border bg-gray-50 text-gray-700 text-sm md:text-base">
      <b>How to read these plots.</b> Each plot is measured at the <i>input to a ReLU</i>, which in the BN model is the BN layer's output—so "BN centers the signal" is partly true by design; the interesting part is how messy the no-BN model gets in comparison. The gradient and update plots add up every parameter in a block, and the two models don't have exactly the same parameters (BN adds <i>γ</i>, <i>β</i>), so compare the <i>shape and steadiness</i> of the curves rather than exact heights. Everything comes from a single training run (one random seed), and the "validation" numbers are measured on CIFAR-10's test set. When we quote a <b>median</b> it's the typical value over training, <b>p95</b> is a near-worst case (the 95th percentile), and a <b>band</b> is the range most values fall in.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">Quick visual: accuracy at a glance</h2>
    <figure class="bg-white rounded border p-2 mb-2">
      <img src="/plots/bn/val acc compare.png" alt="Validation accuracy comparison BN vs No‑BN across learning rates" class="w-full rounded" />
    </figure>
    <p class="text-gray-700">Each curve is accuracy over the course of training. The BN models climb faster and finish higher—and at the aggressive learning rate (lr = 0.2) the no-BN model barely gets off the ground.</p>
    <div class="mt-3 text-base md:text-lg text-gray-700">
      <b>Numbers:</b> lr=0.2 → <span class="text-emerald-700">BN 89.19%</span> vs <span class="text-rose-700">No‑BN 75.14%</span>; lr=0.02 → BN 85.94% vs No‑BN 70.91%.
    </div>
  </section>

  

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">Episode 1 — Activation drift</h2>
<p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">Just before each ReLU, a block produces a set of numbers we'll call the <b>pre-activations</b>—the raw material the ReLU and the next block work with. A healthy sign is that their average stays near 0 and their spread (standard deviation) stays moderate and steady. If the average drifts far from 0, or the spread keeps growing, every later layer has to keep readjusting to a moving target, which makes learning harder. The two carousels below show the mean and the standard deviation of these pre-activations, block by block, over training.</p>
    <div class="mb-4">
      <div class="not-prose bn-carousel" data-images='["/plots/bn/act mean pre relu block 1.png","/plots/bn/act mean pre relu block 2.png","/plots/bn/act mean pre relu block 3.png","/plots/bn/act mean pre relu block 4.png","/plots/bn/act mean pre relu block 5.png"]' data-captions='["Mean (pre‑ReLU), block 1","Mean (pre‑ReLU), block 2","Mean (pre‑ReLU), block 3","Mean (pre‑ReLU), block 4","Mean (pre‑ReLU), block 5"]'></div>
    </div>
    <div class="mb-8">
      <div class="not-prose bn-carousel" data-images='["/plots/bn/act std pre relu block 1.png","/plots/bn/act std pre relu block 2.png","/plots/bn/act std pre relu block 3.png","/plots/bn/act std pre relu block 4.png","/plots/bn/act std pre relu block 5.png"]' data-captions='["Std (pre‑ReLU), block 1","Std (pre‑ReLU), block 2","Std (pre‑ReLU), block 3","Std (pre‑ReLU), block 4","Std (pre‑ReLU), block 5"]'></div>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 rounded border bg-amber-50 text-amber-900">
      <b>What to look for:</b> without BN at lr = 0.2, the deeper blocks drift—in block 5 the average pulls away from 0 (typical magnitude ≈ 2.3, with spikes past 4) and the spread swells (≈ 0.9–2.2). With BN, the average stays pinned near 0 (under ~0.4) and the spread stays tight (≈ 0.35–0.70). Keep in mind BN sits right before this measurement, so it's expected to look tidy here; the takeaway is how far the no-BN version wanders.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">Episode 2 — Dead activations</h2>
<p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">ReLU sets every negative input to 0. That's fine in moderation, but if a unit's pre-activations sit mostly below 0, it outputs 0 most of the time—and a unit stuck at 0 passes almost no gradient backward, so it barely learns. The plot shows the fraction of pre-activations below 0 (the share ReLU zeroes out) for each block. By keeping pre-activations centered near 0, BN preserves a healthier mix of "firing" and "silent" units. (This is measured per batch, so it's about momentary sparsity, not units that are permanently dead.)</p>
    <div class="mb-6">
      <div class="not-prose bn-carousel" data-images='["/plots/bn/fraction inactive pre relu block 1.png","/plots/bn/fraction inactive pre relu block 2.png","/plots/bn/fraction inactive pre relu block 3.png","/plots/bn/fraction inactive pre relu block 4.png","/plots/bn/fraction inactive pre relu block 5.png"]' data-captions='["Inactive fraction, block 1","Inactive fraction, block 2","Inactive fraction, block 3","Inactive fraction, block 4","Inactive fraction, block 5"]'></div>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 text-gray-800">
      <b>By the numbers:</b> at lr = 0.2, the typical inactive fraction per block (1→5) is about 0.41, 0.70, 0.70, 0.88, 0.62 with BN versus 0.65, 0.95, 0.85, 0.90, 0.92 without—so the no-BN network silences far more of its units, especially in the deeper blocks. At the gentle lr = 0.02 the gap shrinks but is still there.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">Episode 3 — Gradient noise</h2>
<p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">During backpropagation, each layer receives a gradient that tells it how to change its weights; the <b>gradient norm</b> is simply the size of that signal. Ideally the sizes are similar across depth and don't swing wildly from step to step—otherwise some layers take huge steps while others barely move. The plot tracks the gradient norm per block over training; BN tends to keep these sizes steadier.</p>
    <div class="mb-6">
      <div class="not-prose bn-carousel" data-images='["/plots/bn/grad norm block 1.png","/plots/bn/grad norm block 2.png","/plots/bn/grad norm block 3.png","/plots/bn/grad norm block 4.png","/plots/bn/grad norm block 5.png","/plots/bn/grad norm head.png"]' data-captions='["Grad norm, block 1","Grad norm, block 2","Grad norm, block 3","Grad norm, block 4","Grad norm, block 5","Grad norm, head"]'></div>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 rounded border bg-rose-50 text-rose-900">
      <b>Look closely:</b> at lr = 0.2 the no-BN curves—especially the head and deeper blocks—jump around and spike more, while the BN curves stay in a calmer range (typical sizes ≈ 0.29–0.50 with BN vs ≈ 0.46–0.64 without). These are raw magnitudes and BN slightly changes which parameters a block contains, so focus on how steady each curve is rather than the exact heights.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">Episode 4 — Update scale</h2>
<p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">A handy sanity check is the <b>relative update size</b>: how much a block's weights change in one step compared with how big they already are (written ‖ΔW‖ / ‖W‖). Too small and the layer is barely learning; too large and it's lurching around. You'd also like it to be roughly similar across blocks, so no single layer dominates. BN tends to pull these into a tighter, more uniform range.</p>
    <div class="mb-6">
      <div class="not-prose bn-carousel" data-images='["/plots/bn/rel update size block 1.png","/plots/bn/rel update size block 2.png","/plots/bn/rel update size block 3.png","/plots/bn/rel update size block 4.png","/plots/bn/rel update size block 5.png","/plots/bn/rel update size head.png"]' data-captions='["Rel. update size, block 1","Rel. update size, block 2","Rel. update size, block 3","Rel. update size, block 4","Rel. update size, block 5","Rel. update size, head"]'></div>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 text-gray-800">
      <b>By the numbers:</b> at lr = 0.2 the BN updates sit in a tight range (~0.008–0.009), while the no-BN updates are smaller and more uneven (~0.006–0.007). At lr = 0.02 the two are similar, with BN a bit more uniform across depth.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">Episode 5 — Batch‑to‑batch shift</h2>
<p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">Each layer sees a slightly different input from one mini-batch to the next. We measure how much a block's average activation jumps between consecutive batches—call it the <b>batch-to-batch shift</b>. Big jumps mean the optimizer is partly chasing a moving target and wasting steps. BN dampens these jumps, so each step sees a more consistent picture.</p>
    <div class="mb-6">
      <div class="not-prose bn-carousel" data-images='["/plots/bn/batch to batch act shift bn-lr0.02.png","/plots/bn/batch to batch act shift Nobn-lr0.02.png","/plots/bn/batch to batch act shift bn-lr0.2.png","/plots/bn/batch to batch act shift Nobn-lr0.2.png"]' data-captions='["BN, lr=0.02","No‑BN, lr=0.02","BN, lr=0.2","No‑BN, lr=0.2"]'></div>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 rounded border bg-emerald-50 text-emerald-900">
      <b>By the numbers:</b> the average shift over training at lr = 0.2 is about 0.014 with BN versus 0.225 without—roughly 16× smaller. At lr = 0.02 it's 0.023 vs 0.085.
    </div>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 items-start mt-6">
      <figure class="bg-white rounded border p-2">
        <img src="/plots/bn/batch to batch act shift Nobn-lr0.2.png" alt="No‑BN, lr=0.2: batch‑to‑batch activation shift" class="w-full rounded" />
        <figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-600 mt-1">No‑BN: larger, choppy shifts</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="bg-white rounded border p-2">
        <img src="/plots/bn/batch to batch act shift bn-lr0.2.png" alt="BN, lr=0.2: batch‑to‑batch activation shift" class="w-full rounded" />
        <figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-600 mt-1">BN: calmer, smoother shifts</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 text-gray-800 mt-3">
      <b>Same learning rate, very different outcome (lr = 0.2):</b> the no-BN run has much larger batch-to-batch shift (≈ 0.225) and stalls near 75% accuracy, while the BN run is far steadier (≈ 0.014) and reaches ~89%. They clearly move together—but this is a single run, and "less shift" has not been shown to be the <i>cause</i> (recall the note above: the better-supported reason is a smoother loss surface).
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">Takeaways</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-700 space-y-1">
      <li>BN rescales the signals inside each layer, keeping activations and gradients in a steadier range.</li>
      <li>That stability lets you use a larger learning rate and train faster, with less babysitting.</li>
      <li>Deep layers benefit most—without BN they're the first to drift and get noisy.</li>
      <li>BN's real benefit probably isn't about "covariate shift"; the better-supported explanation is a smoother, easier-to-optimize loss surface.</li>
      <li>Heads-up: BN relies on batch statistics, so very small batches make it noisy. In that case try GroupNorm or LayerNorm, which don't depend on batch size.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div class="mt-10 flex items-center gap-3">
    <a href="/demos/batchnorm" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-5 py-3 rounded bg-blue-600 text-white hover:bg-blue-700 text-base md:text-lg">Explore the interactive dashboard</a>
    <a href="https://github.com/eitamar-saraf/BN-vs-NonBN" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-5 py-3 rounded border text-blue-700 hover:bg-blue-50 text-base md:text-lg">Repository</a>
  </div>

  <p class="mt-6 text-base md:text-lg text-gray-600">A caveat to keep you honest: these plots come from a single training run each, so they show trends, not error bars. For a claim you could rely on, you'd repeat the experiment with several random seeds and average the results.</p>

  <style is:global>
    /* Coverflow carousel — breaks out wider than the blog column so plot text stays legible */
    .bn-carousel{position:relative;left:50%;transform:translateX(-50%);width:min(1180px,96vw);margin:8px 0}
    .bn-carousel:focus-visible{outline:none}
    .bn-cf__viewport{position:relative;overflow:hidden;padding:16px 0}
    .bn-cf__track{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:22px;transition:transform .42s cubic-bezier(.22,.61,.36,1);will-change:transform}
    .bn-cf__slide{flex:0 0 auto;width:min(900px,72vw);border:1px solid #e5e7eb;border-radius:12px;overflow:hidden;background:#fff;box-shadow:0 1px 3px rgba(0,0,0,.08);transform:scale(.84);opacity:.45;transition:transform .42s cubic-bezier(.22,.61,.36,1),opacity .42s ease,box-shadow .3s ease;cursor:pointer}
    .bn-cf__slide.is-active{transform:scale(1);opacity:1;box-shadow:0 10px 30px rgba(0,0,0,.18);cursor:zoom-in}
    .bn-cf__img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;background:#f8fafc;margin:0}
    .bn-cf__zoom{position:absolute;top:10px;right:10px;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:6px;padding:5px 10px;border-radius:9999px;background:rgba(17,24,39,.72);color:#fff;font-size:.75rem;font-weight:500;opacity:0;transition:opacity .2s ease;pointer-events:none}
    .bn-cf__slide.is-active:hover .bn-cf__zoom{opacity:1}
    .bn-cf__btn{position:absolute;top:50%;transform:translateY(-50%);z-index:5;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;width:46px;height:46px;border:1px solid #e5e7eb;border-radius:9999px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.92);color:#374151;font-size:24px;line-height:1;cursor:pointer;box-shadow:0 2px 6px rgba(0,0,0,.15);transition:background .15s ease,transform .1s ease}
    .bn-cf__btn:hover{background:#fff}
    .bn-cf__btn:active{transform:translateY(-50%) scale(.92)}
    .bn-cf__btn--prev{left:14px}
    .bn-cf__btn--next{right:14px}
    .bn-cf__bar{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;gap:16px;margin-top:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;text-align:center}
    .bn-cf__caption{font-size:.9rem;color:#4b5563;font-weight:500}
    .bn-cf__dots{display:flex;gap:7px}
    .bn-cf__dot{width:9px;height:9px;padding:0;border:0;border-radius:9999px;background:#d1d5db;cursor:pointer;transition:background .15s ease,transform .15s ease}
    .bn-cf__dot:hover{background:#9ca3af}
    .bn-cf__dot--active{background:#2563eb;transform:scale(1.3)}
    .bn-cf__count{font-size:.8rem;color:#6b7280;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;white-space:nowrap}
    @media (max-width:640px){.bn-cf__slide{width:86vw}.bn-cf__btn{width:38px;height:38px;font-size:20px}}
    /* Lightbox — full-resolution view so axis text is readable */
    .bn-lb{position:fixed;inset:0;z-index:1000;display:none;align-items:center;justify-content:center;background:rgba(0,0,0,.88);padding:24px}
    .bn-lb.is-open{display:flex}
    .bn-lb__img{max-width:96vw;max-height:86vh;object-fit:contain;border-radius:8px;background:#fff;box-shadow:0 10px 40px rgba(0,0,0,.5)}
    .bn-lb__cap{position:absolute;bottom:18px;left:0;right:0;text-align:center;color:#e5e7eb;font-size:.9rem;padding:0 16px}
    .bn-lb__btn{position:absolute;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;border:0;border-radius:9999px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.14);color:#fff;cursor:pointer;transition:background .15s ease}
    .bn-lb__btn:hover{background:rgba(255,255,255,.28)}
    .bn-lb__close{top:18px;right:20px;width:42px;height:42px;font-size:24px}
    .bn-lb__nav{top:50%;transform:translateY(-50%);width:52px;height:52px;font-size:30px}
    .bn-lb__prev{left:20px}
    .bn-lb__next{right:20px}
  </style>

  <script type="module">
    // Shared full-resolution lightbox, created once and reused by every carousel.
    const lightbox = (() => {
      const root = document.createElement('div');
      root.className = 'bn-lb';
      const img = document.createElement('img');
      img.className = 'bn-lb__img';
      const cap = document.createElement('div');
      cap.className = 'bn-lb__cap';
      const close = document.createElement('button');
      close.type = 'button'; close.className = 'bn-lb__btn bn-lb__close';
      close.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Close'); close.textContent = '×';
      const prev = document.createElement('button');
      prev.type = 'button'; prev.className = 'bn-lb__btn bn-lb__nav bn-lb__prev';
      prev.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Previous image'); prev.textContent = '‹';
      const next = document.createElement('button');
      next.type = 'button'; next.className = 'bn-lb__btn bn-lb__nav bn-lb__next';
      next.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Next image'); next.textContent = '›';
      root.append(img, cap, close, prev, next);
      document.body.appendChild(root);

      let imgs = [], caps = [], i = 0;
      const wrap = (n) => (n + imgs.length) % imgs.length;
      function show(){
        img.src = imgs[i];
        img.alt = caps[i] || '';
        cap.textContent = (caps[i] ? `${caps[i]} — ` : '') + `${i + 1} / ${imgs.length}`;
        const single = imgs.length <= 1;
        prev.style.display = single ? 'none' : '';
        next.style.display = single ? 'none' : '';
      }
      function closeLb(){ root.classList.remove('is-open'); document.body.style.overflow = ''; }
      close.addEventListener('click', closeLb);
      prev.addEventListener('click', (e) => { e.stopPropagation(); i = wrap(i - 1); show(); });
      next.addEventListener('click', (e) => { e.stopPropagation(); i = wrap(i + 1); show(); });
      root.addEventListener('click', (e) => { if (e.target === root) closeLb(); });
      document.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
        if (!root.classList.contains('is-open')) return;
        if (e.key === 'Escape') closeLb();
        if (e.key === 'ArrowLeft') { i = wrap(i - 1); show(); }
        if (e.key === 'ArrowRight') { i = wrap(i + 1); show(); }
      });
      return {
        open(images, captions, index){
          imgs = images; caps = captions; i = index; show();
          root.classList.add('is-open'); document.body.style.overflow = 'hidden';
        }
      };
    })();

    function initCarousel(el){
      const imgs = JSON.parse(el.getAttribute('data-images') || '[]');
      const caps = JSON.parse(el.getAttribute('data-captions') || '[]');
      if (!imgs.length) return;
      el.replaceChildren();

      const viewport = document.createElement('div');
      viewport.className = 'bn-cf__viewport';
      const track = document.createElement('div');
      track.className = 'bn-cf__track';

      const slides = imgs.map((src, idx) => {
        const slide = document.createElement('div');
        slide.className = 'bn-cf__slide';
        const img = document.createElement('img');
        img.className = 'bn-cf__img';
        img.src = src;
        img.alt = caps[idx] || `Image ${idx + 1} of ${imgs.length}`;
        img.loading = 'lazy';
        img.draggable = false;
        const zoom = document.createElement('span');
        zoom.className = 'bn-cf__zoom';
        zoom.textContent = '⤢ Click to enlarge';
        slide.append(img, zoom);
        slide.addEventListener('click', () => {
          if (idx === current) lightbox.open(imgs, caps, current);
          else { current = idx; render(); }
        });
        track.appendChild(slide);
        return slide;
      });
      viewport.appendChild(track);

      const prev = document.createElement('button');
      prev.type = 'button'; prev.className = 'bn-cf__btn bn-cf__btn--prev';
      prev.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Previous image'); prev.textContent = '‹';
      const next = document.createElement('button');
      next.type = 'button'; next.className = 'bn-cf__btn bn-cf__btn--next';
      next.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Next image'); next.textContent = '›';
      viewport.append(prev, next);

      const bar = document.createElement('div'); bar.className = 'bn-cf__bar';
      const caption = document.createElement('div'); caption.className = 'bn-cf__caption';
      const dots = document.createElement('div'); dots.className = 'bn-cf__dots';
      const count = document.createElement('span'); count.className = 'bn-cf__count';
      const dotEls = imgs.map((_, idx) => {
        const dot = document.createElement('button');
        dot.type = 'button'; dot.className = 'bn-cf__dot';
        dot.setAttribute('aria-label', `Show image ${idx + 1}`);
        dot.addEventListener('click', () => { current = idx; render(); });
        dots.appendChild(dot);
        return dot;
      });
      bar.append(caption, dots, count);
      el.append(viewport, bar);

      let current = 0;
      const wrap = (n) => (n + imgs.length) % imgs.length;
      function center(){
        const slide = slides[current];
        const offset = slide.offsetLeft + slide.offsetWidth / 2 - viewport.clientWidth / 2;
        track.style.transform = `translateX(${-offset}px)`;
      }
      function render(){
        slides.forEach((s, idx) => s.classList.toggle('is-active', idx === current));
        dotEls.forEach((d, idx) => d.classList.toggle('bn-cf__dot--active', idx === current));
        caption.textContent = caps[current] || '';
        count.textContent = `${current + 1} / ${imgs.length}`;
        const single = imgs.length <= 1;
        prev.style.display = single ? 'none' : '';
        next.style.display = single ? 'none' : '';
        dots.style.display = single ? 'none' : '';
        center();
      }

      prev.addEventListener('click', () => { current = wrap(current - 1); render(); });
      next.addEventListener('click', () => { current = wrap(current + 1); render(); });
      el.setAttribute('tabindex', '0');
      el.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
        if (e.key === 'ArrowLeft') { current = wrap(current - 1); render(); }
        if (e.key === 'ArrowRight') { current = wrap(current + 1); render(); }
      });

      // Image sizes change layout as they load; recenter then and on resize.
      slides.forEach((s) => {
        const im = s.querySelector('img');
        if (!im.complete) im.addEventListener('load', center, { once: true });
      });
      let scheduled;
      window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
        cancelAnimationFrame(scheduled);
        scheduled = requestAnimationFrame(center);
      });

      render();
    }
    document.querySelectorAll('.bn-carousel').forEach(initCarousel);
  </script>
</BlogPostLayout>
